The attached image (Figure 4) illustrates that Nano-11 nanoparticles are actively transported to the draining lymph nodes by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) following intramuscular injection. Panel A shows Nano-11 detection in various myeloid cell subtypes, while Panel B reveals that the percentage of Nano-11-containing myeloid cells peaks at Day 2 post-injection, diminishing by Day 7. Panel C demonstrates that migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) consistently contain the highest proportion of Nano-11, and Panel D indicates that MigDCs also exhibit the highest mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) per cell, suggesting greater Nano-11 uptake by these highly immunopotentiating cells compared to lymphoid tissue resident DCs (LTDCs) and monocytes/macrophages (Mo/MΦ). This highlights Nano-11's efficient targeting to key immune cells for adjuvant function.